<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tell Kazel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tell Kazel is an archaeological mound in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkar">Akkar</a> plain of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, near the Lebanese border and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast. Excavations since the 1980s have uncovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> levels, and most scholars identify the site with Sumur, the Simyra of classical sources. Sumur appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> of the 14th century BCE as an Egyptian base contested by the rulers of Amurru.</p></div>
